    "lineage": null,
    "body": {
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "A tree at the centre",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "Yggdrasil, the world tree, has branches that reach across the world and above the sky [1]. Norse sources picture the Nine Worlds around this tree, giving you a way to picture their universe [2]. A squirrel carries an eagle’s words down the tree to a creature below [3]. A dragon gnaws at the tree’s roots [4]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Gods, humans, and the dead",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "Asgard is a world of gods [5]. Midgard is the world where humans live [5]. Hel is a world of the dead, associated at first with those who died from illness or old age [5]. These are three of the Nine Worlds in the account associated with the writer Snorri Sturluson and those who followed him [5]."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The roots reach different places",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "kind": "prose",
              "text": "One poem places Hel, giants, and human lands beneath Yggdrasil’s three roots [3]. A prose account also gives the tree three roots, but includes a root among the gods as well as a root among giants [1]. Yggdrasil stands at the centre of the worlds, while accounts differ about where its roots reach [5][3][1]."
            },
            {
              "kind": "media",
              "caption": "The Course’s own illustration shows the world tree, three roots, and the beings around it.",
              "alt": "A spreading tree has three roots above areas labelled Hel, Giants, and Human lands. An eagle sits high in the tree, a squirrel is lower down, and a dragon beneath the tree gnaws a root.",
